  "prompt_text": "The figure organizes the idea of chirality into two broad categories under the general statement that a chiral object lacks improper rotational symmetry. On the left, it shows geometric or static chirality, where handedness is determined purely by shape. A shoe is used as the familiar example of handed chirality: its mirror image is a distinct left or right form. In contrast, a potato represents nonhanded geometric chirality, suggesting an irregular shape that is still not superimposable on its mirror image but does not form a clear left–right pair in the same intuitive way.\n\nOn the right, the diagram introduces motion-based or dynamic chirality, where chirality depends on how an object moves rather than only on its static geometry. A rotating and translating sphere is labeled as true chirality, implying that its combination of spin and motion creates genuinely distinct left- and right-handed states. By comparison, a rotating cone is labeled false chirality, indicating that although it may look chiral during motion, a time evolution involving a half-turn appears to connect one apparent handed state to the other. Overall, the figure contrasts static shape chirality with chirality emerging from motion, and distinguishes genuine dynamic handedness from only apparent handedness.",
